## Readability rules (apply everywhere in prose)

**Goal:** Short, linear sentences. A tired reader should never parse nested asides.

### Punctuation — strict

| Avoid | Use instead |
|-------|-------------|
| Parentheses `(…)` | Separate sentence, comma clause, or em-dash — **at most one pair per paragraph**, and zero in Abstract |
| Semicolons `;` | Full stop. Start a new sentence |
| Colons `:` in running prose | Full stop or “namely” / “that is” in plain words. Colons OK only in figure caption panel labels **A,** **B,** and in `\methodpara{Label.}` |
| Stacked em-dashes | One em-dash per paragraph maximum |
| `(i)(ii)(iii)` inline lists | Numbered sentences or “First, … Second, …” |
| SI/table refs in parentheses | “see S3 Table” or `\nameref{S3_Table}` in the sentence |

### Sentence shape

- **One idea per sentence.** Target 12–22 words; split anything longer.
- **One idea per paragraph.** 3–5 sentences; never 8+.
- **Lead with the claim**, then the number, then why it matters.
- **No protocol laundry lists in one sentence.** Cohort size, labs, folds, and metric belong in separate sentences if needed.
- **Define acronyms in prose** — “electroencephalography, EEG” on first use — not `(EEG)`.

### Words and patterns to cut

- “novel in combining”, “offers a path to”, “threefold contributions”, “it is important to note”
- Repeated AccuSleep / transductive calibration contrast — **once in Intro, once in Methods**
- “placeholder”, “provisional”, “pending curation”, “thesis-like”, repo file paths in tables
- Double fillers: “As shown in Table X, the results confirm…”

### Before / after (readability)

**Bad — clutter:**
> On a quality-screened MSSV cohort (20 wild-type mice, laboratories 2/3/5, four-fold leave-mice-out cross-validation), decoder-only cVAEs stage held-out animals from spectral inputs alone (no subject IDs; no manual baseline at inference).

**Good — linear:**
> We study twenty quality-screened wild-type mice from three European MSSV laboratories under leave-mice-out cross-validation with four folds. Decoder-only conditional variational autoencoders assign states from spectral EEG and EMG alone. At inference the model uses only the encoder and mixture prior. It does not use subject identifiers or a manually scored calibration period.

**Bad — semicolon chain:**
> NMI falls as K rises; log-likelihood improves; therefore K is chosen for biology.

**Good:**
> Holdout macro NMI falls as K rises. Prior log-likelihood still improves. We therefore choose K for biological interpretability, not from macro NMI alone.

